From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RJ Skerry-Ryan <rryan@alum.mit.edu>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:00:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: apple_cpu: Power gate CPU clusters with no online CPU

Once every core of a CPU cluster is offline, the cluster can be powered
down as a whole. Its CPU complex (ACC) loses its configuration and must
be set up again before a core runs in it, and its cores can then only be
started through the PMGR, once each per cluster power cycle: a core is
never started again on its own.

With apple_cpu.cluster_gating (on by default, also writable at runtime),
when the last online core of a cluster goes offline:

- cpu_disable first gives every parked core of the cluster the
  power-down command over a fast IPI: the core cleans its L1 data cache
  by set/way, sets its PMGR stop request and WFIs without state
  retention. A parked core already reads run state 0, so the controlling
  core waits for the core's bit in the PMGR stop control, then for run
  state 0. Only once every other core is down does it capture the
  cluster's ACC feature bits and p-state, move the cluster to the lowest
  p-state of its OPP table and turn the AMX/LLC/power throttles and the
  APSC off, in that order, waiting for the APSC to settle; the CPU
  complex is never quiesced under a core that still has to wake from
  its park. If a parked core does not power down, the cluster is not
  gated and the last core parks too; cores already powered down stay
  down until the cluster is next power cycled. If the CPU complex does
  not settle, the cluster is gated anyway, since it is re-initialised
  from the captured configuration on power-up;
- cpu_die additionally lets the CPU complex enter deep sleep (ACC_OVRD
  deep sleep, L2 flush on deep sleep, SRM/link/CPM power-down, no PIO on
  WFI), which is only safe from the last core of the cluster, and powers
  the core down through the PMGR;
- cpu_kill cannot poll the core status since the cluster's registers stop
  responding, so it waits 10ms and sets the cluster's PMGR power state
  target to off. Every core of the cluster can then be started once.

Before the first core of a gated cluster starts, cpu_boot sets the
cluster's PMGR power state target to on and waits for it to settle, then
re-initialises the CPU complex: APSC p-state, the captured feature bits
(APSC, power/LLC/AMX throttles, fixed-frequency PLL relock), the ACC init
and APSC snooze controls, and finally the captured p-state. The core and
the other cores of the cluster are then started through the PMGR. The
first core back restores ACC_OVRD.

A started core runs the boot loader's resident secondary entry path,
which on m1n1 prints to its console UART and its dockchannel UART before
it reaches the spin table, polling their status registers. Linux may
have powered those blocks down, and such an access hangs the machine.
So clusters are only gated when that path is known to stay quiet:

- if the boot loader publishes the warm boot mailbox in
  /chosen/asahi,s2r-mailbox, its QUIET flag is set before each start;
- otherwise the resident image must be m1n1 v1.6.1, identified by
  /chosen/asahi,m1n1-stage2-version, by every secondary's
  cpu-release-addr pointing at a slot of its spin table at P+0xe0240 of
  a page-aligned base P outside System RAM, by its reset entry and by the
  code of both UART put functions, which load the base pointers at
  P+0xe2d78 and P+0xd8030, and by those pointers holding serial0's
  address and the dockchannel UART's, or 0. Both pointers are cleared
  before each start; the put functions then return at once.

Any other boot loader is logged at boot, and its clusters are never
gated: the last core of a cluster parks like the others.

The cluster power states live in the PMGR syscon, referenced with
apple,pmgr; the CPU complex registers come from the accN regions of the
cpu-ctrl node. Clusters missing either are never gated, nor are clusters
with a core that never booted or is in an unknown state.

Suspend to RAM now takes the secondaries offline the same way, instead
of stopping each core on its own: a core stopped alone can only be
started again after its cluster was power cycled, so an aborted suspend
left every secondary unable to come back. Each cluster without the boot
CPU is gated by its last core; the boot CPU's cluster keeps its
secondaries parked, so an abort unparks them.

For the system suspend path, export:
- apple_cpu_acc_save(), which captures the CPU complex configuration of
  the powered clusters, and apple_cpu_acc_restore(), which re-applies it
  after they lost power. A cluster whose re-init is still pending (a
  failed start) keeps its earlier capture, and apple_cpu_acc_restore()
  completes its re-init;
- apple_cpu_park_power_down_all(), which powers the parked cores down,
  since they would not survive their cluster losing power. It must
  run before any other SoC power is taken down, since a parked core
  must not be woken after that (the same order as cluster gating).
  Past it, an aborted suspend leaves those CPUs offline until reboot;
- apple_cpu_power_cycled(), called on resume, after which every
  powered-down core can be started once.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: RJ Skerry-Ryan <rryan@alum.mit.edu>
